1. Elebsiran plus tobevibart establishes a Phase 3 path toward deep hepatitis=
 delta suppression
What changed
In the 124-patient ECLIPSE 1 trial, monthly elebsiran plus tobevibart produce=
d undetectable hepatitis delta virus RNA and normalized ALT in 44% of partici=
pants at week 48, versus 0% in the delayed-treatment group at week 12. Sevent=
y-nine percent achieved undetectable virus, and all met the broader virologic=
-response definition. One grade 3 treatment-related neutrophil decrease occur=
red, with no treatment discontinuations. (
Vir Biotechnology,
Vir Biotechnology Announces Primary Endpoint Met in Pivotal Phase 3 ECLIPSE 1=
 Trial
, November 8, 2026
)
What it tells us
The result validates simultaneous suppression of hepatitis B surface-antigen =
production with siRNA and viral entry or particle clearance with an antibody.=
 Deep suppression across cirrhotic and non-cirrhotic patients strengthens the=
 regulatory case. The design compares different treatment durations rather th=
an equal-time concurrent therapy, however, and the endpoints remain virologic=
 and biochemical rather than decompensation, liver cancer or mortality.
Commercially, monthly administration could offer meaningful convenience. Glob=
al differentiation still depends on direct performance against bulevirtide.
What to watch next
ECLIPSE 2 and the head-to-head ECLIPSE 3 results, durability after treatment =
withdrawal and the planned mid-2027 FDA submission.
2. Early TAVR reduces heart-failure events in selected moderate aortic stenos=
is
What changed
PROGRESS randomized 744 patients with moderate aortic stenosis and at least o=
ne symptom or structural risk feature to Edwards=E2=80=99 balloon-expandable =
TAVR or surveillance. At two years, death or a heart-failure event occurred i=
n 16.7% versus 23.9%, an absolute difference of 7.2 percentage points. Heart-=
failure events fell from 19.0% to 10.8%, while mortality was 8.4% with TAVR a=
nd 7.3% with surveillance. More than half the surveillance group underwent va=
lve replacement during follow-up. (
American College of Cardiology,
PROGRESS: TAVR Superior to Clinical Surveillance in Patients With Moderate AS
, November 2, 2026
;
New England Journal of Medicine,
TAVR in Patients with Moderate Aortic Stenosis with At-Risk Features
, November 2, 2026
)
What it tells us
PROGRESS supports earlier intervention for a selected, predominantly symptoma=
tic population whose disease frequently progresses within two years. The bene=
fit is morbidity reduction and improved health status, not longer survival. T=
hat distinction matters when weighing procedural risk, valve durability and c=
ost.
Commercial significance could be substantial because moderate disease is outs=
ide the current U.S. indication. Edwards gains first-mover evidence, although=
 broader category expansion would also benefit competing TAVR manufacturers.
What to watch next
FDA label strategy, guideline changes, reimbursement and whether longer follo=
w-up shows durability or a mortality difference.
3. Pelacarsen and ziltivekimab show that biomarker control does not establish=
 cardiovascular benefit
What changed
Detailed Lp(a)HORIZON results showed that pelacarsen lowered lipoprotein(a) b=
y approximately 78%, yet cardiovascular events occurred in 12.3% of treated p=
atients and 12.1% of controls (hazard ratio 1.02). Even among patients with b=
aseline Lp(a) of at least 90 mg/dL, event rates were identical at 12.3% (haza=
rd ratio 1.00). (
New England Journal of Medicine,
Pelacarsen and Cardiovascular Outcomes in Patients with High Lipoprotein(a)
, November 7, 2026
)
ZEUS produced the same disconnect: ziltivekimab inhibited interleukin-6 and l=
owered inflammatory markers but did not reduce cardiovascular events in patie=
nts with atherosclerosis and chronic kidney disease (hazard ratio 0.99). (
New England Journal of Medicine,
Interleukin-6 Inhibition with Ziltivekimab for Atherosclerotic Disease
, November 7, 2026
)
What it tells us
The new data resolve whether obvious subgroups could rescue either program: n=
one did. Pelacarsen=E2=80=99s failure weakens, without refuting, the broader =
Lp(a)-lowering thesis because competing siRNAs may achieve different depth, d=
uration or biology. ZEUS similarly tests one antibody in a severely ill popul=
ation rather than disproving cardiovascular inflammation as a target.
Both results raise the commercial evidentiary bar: biomarker magnitude cannot=
 substitute for outcomes trials.
What to watch next
Event data for olpasiran and lepodisiran, and analyses explaining why genetic=
 risk associations failed to translate pharmacologically.
4. FDA advances AMT-130 as confirmed Zolgensma insertional oncogenesis raises=
 the AAV safety bar
What changed
FDA accepted uniQure=E2=80=99s AMT-130 Huntington=E2=80=99s disease BLA for a=
ccelerated approval and set May 1, 2027 as the target decision date. The appl=
ication relies on 36-month results from 12 high-dose patients compared with a=
n external control. At 48 months, the 12-patient cUHDRS analysis was not stat=
istically significant, although total functional capacity favored treatment n=
ominally; a randomized 200-patient confirmatory trial is planned. (
uniQure,
uniQure Announces Third Quarter 2026 Financial Results and Provides Company U=
pdate
, November 4, 2026
)
Separately, FDA confirmed two Zolgensma tumor cases in which vector DNA integ=
rated near or within the GLI1 oncogene. The agency extended the identified ne=
oplasia risk to Itvisma, which uses the same vector and construct, while main=
taining that both products=E2=80=99 benefits outweigh their risks. (
FDA,
FDA Investigating Serious Risk of Malignancy Linked to Zolgensma Gene Therapy
, November 6, 2026
)
What it tells us
BLA acceptance confirms regulatory willingness to consider sparse, externally=
 controlled evidence in fatal neurodegeneration; it does not resolve AMT-130=
=E2=80=99s efficacy uncertainty. The Zolgensma findings convert insertional o=
ncogenesis from a theoretical label risk into a demonstrated product-related =
event. The appropriate read-through is stricter construct-specific integratio=
n analysis and long-term surveillance, rather than a class-wide conclusion ab=
out all AAV therapies.
What to watch next
A potential AMT-130 advisory committee, FDA action on Zolgensma labeling and =
tumor-monitoring requirements across related programs.
5. CMS closes a formulation route around Medicare negotiation
What changed
CMS finalized rules governing Medicare drug-price negotiation for prices effe=
ctive in 2029 and beyond. When products from the same application holder shar=
e an active ingredient and an added component creates a new formulation enabl=
ing another administration route, CMS will generally aggregate them as one qu=
alifying single-source drug. CMS explicitly cited products combining an activ=
e ingredient with hyaluronidase as an example. The rule takes effect December=
 5, 2026. (
CMS,
Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program and Medicare Prescription Drug Benefi=
t Program Final Rule
, November 5, 2026
)
What it tells us
The final rule reduces the ability to protect economics by shifting an establ=
ished infused biologic into a subcutaneous fixed combination shortly before n=
egotiation. Convenience, chair-time savings and improved adherence retain cli=
nical and competitive value, but the new formulation may inherit the original=
 product=E2=80=99s maximum fair price exposure.
The policy favors lifecycle strategies that demonstrate additional clinical b=
enefit, reach a distinct population or introduce a genuinely new molecule. It=
 also makes negotiation timing more important in decisions to license formula=
tion technology or fund route-switching trials.
What to watch next
CMS=E2=80=99s February 1, 2027 selection of up to 20 Part B and Part D drugs =
for the 2029 cycle and manufacturers=E2=80=99 responses to the aggregation po=
licy.
